  "summary": "Indian vitreous enamel: jewel colors fused into gold and silver, red the crown of Jaipur work.",
  "description": "Meenakari fuses ground glass onto gold, silver or copper in kiln-fired layers — champlevé and cloisonné cells filled with green, blue, white and above all the deep pigeon-blood red that Jaipur made famous. Brought to maturity when Raja Man Singh brought Persian-trained enamelers to Jaipur in the 1500s, the art enameled sword hilts, ritual thalis, huqqa bases and above all the invisible backs of kundan-set jewelry, where hidden meena glows against the skin. Jaipur's Maniharon ka Rasta lanes still ring with enamel kilns; Lucknow and Varanasi keep blue-work schools.",
  "culturalContext": "The art of enameling metal for ornamental reasons has been traced back to the Parthian and Sasanian eras of Iranian history. However, the meticulous ornamental work seen today can be traced back to Safavid Iran around the 15th century. The Mughals introduced it to India and perfected the technique, making the designs applied on objects more intricate. The craft reached its peak in Iran during the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. In the twentieth century, Iranian artisans specialising in meenakari were invited to other regions to assist with training local craftsmen. In India, Rajasthan and Gujarat are most famous for their Mīnākārī artifacts and jewelry.",
